{- | The verdict, value and diagnostic-code conformance table for
   `docs/spec/DICT-SEMANTICS.md`, over `test/dict_fixtures`.

   Every row pins what the binary does, not what the spec says should
   happen. A gate that asserted the spec would simply be red, and a red
   gate teaches people to ignore it. A divergence is pinned with a label
   naming its issue, so this table doubles as the conformance ledger and
   self-drains: the day a fix lands the row goes red and whoever fixed it
   re-pins the cell.

   The converse is the more dangerous half. A captured value records what
   the engine did, not what is correct. Every `pinned` below was
   hand-derived from the spec first, in the fixture's own header comment,
   and only then compared against the binary. Where they agree the row is
   conformant; where they disagree the label says so and names the issue.
   Three engines agreeing does not prove correctness — several known S0s
   had every engine equally wrong, which is why `diff_compiler_engines`
   cannot see them.

   A row's `label` is load-bearing, not decoration. Several rows are
   numerically indistinguishable from an outcome that was a bug, so the
   label carries why the verdict is what it is and which fix moved it. A
   reader who transcribes the verdicts and paraphrases the labels destroys
   the pin.

   Clause §7's single-evaluator law is a claim about values ("two
   evaluators … must agree, value-for-value, on every elaborated core
   program"), so an `AllExact` row asserts the run engine's stdout, the
   built binary's stdout and `pinned` are all byte-identical. An
   exit-code-only gate is blind to "build exits 0 printing a wrong number",
   which is #607's build-path symptom.

   A rejecting row additionally pins the diagnostic code `check --json`
   reports, or a row could pass by being rejected for an unrelated reason —
   a typo in a fixture would otherwise grade green. `Absent` is the other
   half, and the #1155 closedness pair needs it: its two files differ only
   in whether the goal is closed, both exit nonzero from the same coherence
   reject, and the only observable difference is that the open one must not
   carry `T-AMBIGUOUS-INSTANCE`. Without a negative assertion that pair
   would grade green with the closedness gate deleted.

   Two value modes are live. The shell gate this table was lifted out of
   also carried `BUILD_EXACT` (build's stdout alone, for a row `run`
   rejects by design, optionally written `<run-stdout>%%<build-stdout>` to
   pin how far `run` got before dying) and `SPLIT_EXACT` (a §7 violation
   ledger where both engines accept and must disagree, so the row reds the
   day they converge). Neither had a single row at the time this moved, so
   each is re-expressible as a `Mode` constructor plus a `gradeValue` arm
   rather than carried here ungraded. `Agreement` keeps every stream apart,
   so both remain expressible unchanged.

   No row grades `run`'s stderr. The harness grades `check`'s diagnostic
   code, every engine's stdout and every exit code, but cannot assert a
   runtime panic's signature (#1130), so a row whose pinned failure is a
   run-time panic pins the exit code and the stdout reached, never the
   reason.

   The per-row verdict table is rendered into the failure message rather
   than printed: `medaka test --native` runs a test body inside a probe
   whose stdout it parses back as a sentinel transcript, so anything the
   body prints on its own account never reaches a reader.

   Runs under `medaka test --native`: it spawns subprocesses and reads the
   corpus, neither of which the interpreter binds. -}

import compiler_cli_test_support.{
  Agreement(..), checkRunBuild, runMedaka, summarizeSweep, sweepUnits, verdictOf
}
import fs.{expectUnitCount, fixtureDirs}
import list.{somes, sort}
import path.{basename}
import string.{contains, endsWith, padRight, replaceAll, stripSuffix, unwords}
import test.{Expectation, fail, pass}
import test_process.{medakaRoot, testAssertionCount, unrosteredUnits}

-- Fixtures are located through MEDAKA_ROOT rather than the working
-- directory: `medaka gate run` exports it and inherits whatever cwd the
-- caller had.
corpus : <IO> String
corpus = "\{medakaRoot}/test/dict_fixtures"

{- | What a row asserts about the stdout of a fixture that runs.

   `NoValue` is for a row where nothing ran to completion. `AllExact`
   requires the run engine's stdout, the built binary's stdout and the
   pinned value to be byte-identical. -}
data Mode = NoValue | AllExact

{- | A diagnostic code `medaka check --json` must report for a row, or must
   not report. `Absent` is what makes a pair of fixtures sharing a verdict,
   an exit code and a stdout tell each other apart. -}
-- Not a `Result` clone worth collapsing: neither arm is a failure, both carry
-- the same kind of String, and `Result String String` would leave every one of
-- the table's code literals unable to say which polarity it asserts.
-- lint-disable-next-line rule-clone-type
data CodeAssert = Present String | Absent String

{- | One conformance row: the entry path under the corpus, the label
   carrying why the row reads as it does, the verdict each verb owes, what
   the fixture prints, and the diagnostic codes `check --json` owes. -}
data Row = Row {
  entry : String,
  label : String,
  expCheck : String,
  expRun : String,
  expBuild : String,
  mode : Mode,
  pinned : String,
  codes : List CodeAssert,
}

