      "§8 I6.1 THE S2-5 REGRESSION ROW (S2, introduced by this sprint`s slice 2, fixed by its fix round). The STRUCTURED sibling of test/typecheck_error_fixtures/rigid_mono_head_key.mdk, whose golden is exactly this reject one level up. `zzz` in `data Bad = Bad zzz` is quantified by NOTHING, and §8 I6.1 calls handing such a variable out as a dispatch key a forgery; `monoHeadIsDecl` catches it at the TOP LEVEL of a goal, but nested one constructor down (`Q (Wrap zzz)`) the head is a real TCon and the conjunct is True either way, so the forgery leaked. A rigid that can never ground has nowhere to defer TO. 🚨 THE DISCRIMINATOR IS THE SECOND DIAGNOSTIC, NOT THE EXIT CODE: at the pre-fix head `aa36d555` this file emitted ONE error (T-TYPE-MISMATCH at the call); at the sprint merge-base `0944ca9d` it emitted TWO, that one AND `No impl of Q for Wrap zzz`. Both arms exit 1, so an exit-only row could not have seen the loss -- which is why BOTH codes are asserted here, and why the bare-rigid control (`qq v`, no `Wrap`, still rejecting on both arms) could not have caught it either. ⚠️ ONE impl block, so Section 4 derives no permutation pair -- correct, the defect was a LOST DIAGNOSTIC, not order",
    expCheck = "REJECT",
    expRun = "REJECT",
    expBuild = "REJECT",
    mode = NoValue,
    pinned = "",
    codes = [Present "T-TYPE-MISMATCH", Present "T-NO-IMPL"],
  },
  Row {
    entry = "s-nary-given-declared-vector-selects.mdk",
    label =
      "§3/§5 THE #1956 REGRESSION ROW (S0, live at the sprint base; #1956/#1937 carried NO must_fail pin, so before this row the shape had no guard anywhere in the tree). A MULTI-ARGUMENT structured declared context must select PER CALL SITE from the dict the caller built: `f`s context IS its body goal, so (assum) applies and §3`s precedence clause forbids the body committing to an impl of its own, while each call site discharges its own instantiated goal by (inst). Both positions differ between the two calls (`Conv (Wrap Int) Bool` vs `Conv (Wrap Char) Int`), which is what makes the VECTOR rather than the LEAD the thing that has to select -- the two `s-multiparam-structured-*` rows both fix the second argument to `Int` and cannot see that difference. MEASURED at the sprint base `e4587a04` on this tree, cold built: run 0 printing `wrap-int-bool/wrap-int-bool`, the FIRST impl`s body for both calls, because `registerPredGiven` matched a single-subject `[subject]` vector and returned `()` for anything longer, so `entailAssum` could never see the predicate and `entail` fell to `entailInst`s abstraction-time commitment. 🚨 AND ORDER-DECIDED: with the two `impl Conv` blocks swapped the base printed `wrap-char-int/wrap-char-int`, which §3 forbids outright -- Section 4`s permutation pair grades that half automatically now. ⚠️ THE REJECT SIBLING IS s-nary-given-nonlead-mismatch-rejected.mdk: a matcher keyed on the lead argument alone would answer this goal AND that one, so neither file is meaningful alone",
    expCheck = "ACCEPT",
    expRun = "ACCEPT",
    expBuild = "ACCEPT",
    mode = AllExact,
    pinned = "wrap-int-bool/wrap-char-int\n",
    codes = [],
  },
  Row {
    entry = "s-nary-encl-nonlead-slot-routes.mdk",
    label =
      "§3/§5 THE #1904 REGRESSION ROW (S0, live at the merge-base with main). An enclosing function`s own declared context names ONE interface TWICE at two argument vectors that differ only at NON-LEAD TYVAR positions (`(Ix a b c, Ix a c b)`), and its two body occurrences `ix n x y` / `ix n y x` must read TWO DIFFERENT dict slots: (assum) applies twice, to two DIFFERENT assumptions, so at `size 5 x y` at a=Int,b=Char,c=Bool slot 0 is `Ix Int Char Bool` (333) and slot 1 is `Ix Int Bool Char` (111) and the answer is 444. MEASURED at `44257622` on this tree, cold built: check 0, run 0 and build 0 all printing `666` -- 333 twice, the FIRST slot`s dict for both occurrences, a silent wrong answer at exit 0 on BOTH engines. 🚨 TWO independent head-tycon-only encodings had to move for this row, and either alone leaves it red: the KEY (`oblArgKey` -> `cslotVecKey`) rendered every tyvar position as the constant \"*\", so both predicates keyed alike and `dedupSlotVecs` collapsed them into ONE slot -- there was no slot 1 to route to; and the ROUTING (`predArgsAgree`/`monoHeadAgrees`, reached from `enclDictVarOf` -> `enclSlotIndex` -> `indexOfPredGo` pass 1) treated a tyvar position as agreeing with EVERYTHING, so once slot 1 existed it was allocated and never read. Fixing only the key moves `size`s emitted dict arity 4 -> 5 and changes no answer. ⚠️ NOT ORDER-DECIDED IN THE USUAL WAY: swapping the two `impl Ix` blocks also printed `666` at the base, because the collapse is to the first CONTEXT SLOT, not the first IMPL -- Section 4 grades the permutation for free but the value cell is what sees this defect. ⚠️ THE GROUND-POSITION CONTROL is the `(Ix a Char, Ix a Bool)` shape, which routed correctly even at the base because `monoHeadAgrees` can see a ground head",
    expCheck = "ACCEPT",
    expRun = "ACCEPT",
    expBuild = "ACCEPT",
    mode = AllExact,
    pinned = "444\n",
    codes = [],
  },
  Row {
    entry = "s-nary-truncated-goal-not-ambiguous/main.mdk",
    label =
      "§3 THE #1867 REGRESSION ROW (S1, live at the merge-base with main). `recordCallObligations` records ONE obligation per SHATTERED per-tyvar dict slot, so a call to a two-parameter predicate also records the 1-ary goal `Ix Int` with the second argument DROPPED. That truncation is deliberate (#1161: widening it moves emitted dict arity) and harmless -- until the truncated goal is allowed to DECIDE. It was: `checkOneCallObligation` at arity 1 is `implMatchesReceiverU`, receiver-only, so `Ix Int` matched BOTH `impl Ix Int Char` and `impl Ix Int Bool`, and `checkNestedReqs` posed that 1-ary goal to the min-specific selector, which found two incomparable candidates and reported `T-AMBIGUOUS-INSTANCE` for a program with no ambiguity in it -- the JOINT obligations `Ix Int Char` / `Ix Int Bool` each match exactly one impl by (inst). MEASURED at `44257622` cold built: `check` exit 0 printing `main : Unit` and `check --json` with ZERO diagnostics, while `run` and `build` BOTH exit 1 with the unlocated \"detected during elaboration ... see issue #1812\" surface -- the three verbs DISAGREED, which is why all three columns are asserted. 🚨 IT MUST BE A DIRECTORY FIXTURE: the identical source as ONE module prints 333 at exit 0 even at the base (the diagnostic is recorded there too, but the single-module `run` path never surfaces it), so a flat `.mdk` sibling would have been green at the base and pinned nothing. ⚠️ THE FAIL-CAPABLE HALF is s-nary-truncated-goal-joint-rejects/, without which a fix that deferred the truncated goal and lost the joint one would be green here",
    expCheck = "ACCEPT",
    expRun = "ACCEPT",
    expBuild = "ACCEPT",
    mode = AllExact,
    pinned = "333\n",
    codes = [],
  },
  Row {
    entry = "s-nary-truncated-goal-joint-rejects/main.mdk",
    label =
      "§3/§5 THE FALSE-ACCEPT CANARY for the row above. EXACTLY ONE dimension flipped: the second impl is `Ix Bool Bool`, so the predicate `useIx 5` actually needs -- `Ix Int Bool` -- has no impl, while `impl Ix Int Char` is still present precisely so the TRUNCATED goal `Ix Int` stays satisfiable by receiver-only matching. Deferring a truncated goal is sound ONLY because the JOINT obligation decides in its place; this row is what fails if that stops being true, since a fix that deferred the truncation WITHOUT the joint obligation behind it would turn this into a silent accept -- a `run` invoking a Char-keyed dict at `Bool`, a type-confused dict at exit 0 (§5) -- and the ACCEPT sibling is green under that broken fix too. ⚠️ NOT RED AT THE MERGE-BASE, DELIBERATELY: #1867s fix is a pure REJECTION-NARROWING, so no verdict moves from reject to accept and this program rejects with `T-NO-IMPL` at `44257622` and at head alike; its value is forward-facing, the standing the s-nary-given-nonlead-mismatch-rejected canary has. ⚠️ VERDICT AND CODE, NO LOCATION",
    expCheck = "REJECT",
    expRun = "REJECT",
    expBuild = "REJECT",
    mode = NoValue,
    pinned = "",
    codes = [Present "T-NO-IMPL"],
  },
  Row {
    entry = "s-nary-given-nonlead-mismatch-rejected.mdk",
    label =
      "§3 THE FALSE-ACCEPT CANARY for the row above. A declared context answers a body goal only when it IS that goal -- equal length and structural identity at EVERY position, in order -- so `Conv (Wrap a) Int` does NOT answer `Conv (Wrap a) Bool`: (assum) is inapplicable because the predicate is not in P, and (inst) is inapplicable because `phi` must solve `Wrap a = Wrap Int` against the whole vector. `impl Conv (Wrap Int) Int` is present precisely so the CALLER-side obligation at `f 1 True` is satisfiable and the only thing wrong with the program is the body goal`s NON-LEAD position. A matcher keyed on the lead alone, or one reaching for unification instead of structural identity, would accept this and hand the body a `Conv (Wrap Int) Int` dictionary to invoke at `Bool` -- a type-confused dict (§5) at exit 0. ⚠️ NOT RED AT THE SPRINT BASE, DELIBERATELY: #1956`s fix is a pure ACCEPTANCE WIDENING, so no verdict moves from accept to reject and this row rejects at `e4587a04` and at head alike; its value is forward-facing. ⚠️ VERDICT AND CODE, NO LOCATION",
    expCheck = "REJECT",
    expRun = "REJECT",
    expBuild = "REJECT",
    mode = NoValue,
    pinned = "",
    codes = [Present "T-NO-IMPL"],
  },
  Row {
    entry = "ev-global-table-bystander-unaffected/main.mdk",
    label =
      "[T-GLOBAL-TABLE] FOR THE PUBLISHED EVIDENCE TABLE (#2549 M2) -- the only row in this file whose printed values are chosen to be INDEPENDENT of dispatch. `evidenceRef` (compiler/types/route_key.mdk) is a new program-global table keyed by `EvId`, and every other fixture in this corpus asserts a dispatch answer, so all of them READ it: a table that answered the wrong goal to the RIGHT question would keep every one of them green. Here the graph is deliberately full of the construct -- wmod applies the constrained `cmod.tally` at two types (two `EDictAt` sites publishing real slot-ordered `Sz Int` / `Sz Bool` routes under distinct ordinals), and umods unconstrained same-named `pick`, reached through an EXPLICIT import, is the #739 empty-routes arm (an `EDictAt` with NO entry in the table, the miss that must stay silent) -- while `main` prints ONLY bystander.mdks values. bystander imports nothing, declares no interface, impls nothing and is monomorphic throughout, so no route lookup stands between its source and its answer. SPEC ANSWER hand-derived from the sources before any engine was run: bump (Tally 5) = Tally 6, bump of that = Tally 7, so bystanderValue = 7 and bystanderTotal = 7 * 3 = 21. The dict-bearing bindings are forced but not printed (208 and 3, derived in wmod.mdk, asserted nowhere) so the run genuinely executes the dictionary path it elaborates. The #739 site is carried by the VERDICT, not by a value: its symptom is a phantom obligation recorded off the bare-name collision, which makes run and build OVER-REJECT a valid program, so ACCEPT on all three verbs is what pins it",
    expCheck = "ACCEPT",
    expRun = "ACCEPT",
    expBuild = "ACCEPT",
    mode = AllExact,
    pinned = "7\n21\n",
    codes = [],
  },
]

{- | How many rows `table` owes.

   Written down rather than discovered, so that a table which silently
   SHRANK is red rather than green over whatever is left. An emptied table
   would otherwise sweep zero rows and report success, which is the
   self-no-op the shell gate this replaced guarded against per section with
   an `n == 0` check. Growing the corpus is expected: add the row and raise
   the number in the same commit. -}
tableRows : Int
tableRows = 197

{- | One row's graded outcome. `note` carries a structural failure — a row
   naming a fixture that is not there, a spawn that could not start —
   which has no verdict columns of its own to report. -}
data Graded = Graded {
  entry : String,
  checkV : String,
  runV : String,
  buildV : String,
  grade : String,
  ok : Bool,
  note : String,
}

gradeValue : Mode -> String -> Agreement -> String
gradeValue NoValue _ _ = "-"
gradeValue AllExact pinned a =
  if a.runCode /= 0 || a.buildCode /= 0 then
    "n/a"
  else if a.runOut == a.binOut && a.runOut == pinned then
    "ok"
  else
    "DIFF"

structuralRow : String -> String -> Graded
structuralRow entry note = Graded {
  entry = entry,
  checkV = "-",
  runV = "-",
  buildV = "-",
  grade = "-",
  ok = False,
  note = " \{note}",
}

-- The scratch binary's name has to be unique per row, and an entry path can
-- name a file inside a fixture directory, so the separator becomes part of
-- the name rather than a directory the build would have to create.
binName : String -> String
binName entry = replaceAll "/" "_" entry

{- | The grades that mean "graded, and fine". An ALLOWLIST, deliberately: a
   `Mode` arm added later (which this module's header invites) must be RED
   until it is named here, not green by omission. -}
gradePasses : String -> Bool
gradePasses grade = grade == "-" || grade == "ok" || grade == "n/a"

-- Why `json` does not satisfy one code assertion, or `None` when it does.
-- The needle is the `"code":"…"` field `medaka check --json` emits, matched
-- as a substring of the whole envelope: a diagnostic's code is the one part
-- of that envelope a row is entitled to depend on.
codeProblem : String -> CodeAssert -> Option String
codeProblem json (Present c) =
  if contains "\"code\":\"\{c}\"" json then None else Some "MISSING:\{c}"
codeProblem json (Absent c) =
  if contains "\"code\":\"\{c}\"" json then Some "PRESENT:\{c}" else None

{- | Every code assertion `entry` misses, or an `Err` when `check --json`
   could not be spawned at all.

   A row with no code assertion spawns nothing: the fourth process per row
   is paid for only where a row asks for it. The exit code is deliberately
   not graded here — `checkRunBuild` already pins `check`'s verdict for
   every row, and grading it twice would make a row that pins a code
   stricter than the identical row that does not. -}
codeFailures : List CodeAssert ->
  String ->
  <Exec "_", IO> Result String (List String)
codeFailures [] _ = Ok []
codeFailures cs entrypath = match runMedaka ["check", "--json", entrypath]
  Err e => Err "could not run `medaka check --json`: \{e}"
  Ok (_, json) => Ok (somes (map (codeProblem json) cs))

gradeOne : String -> Row -> <Exec "_", FileRead "_", IO> Graded
gradeOne scratch r =
  let entrypath = "\{corpus}/\{r.entry}"
  let binPath = "\{scratch}/\{binName r.entry}.bin"
  match readFile entrypath
    Err e => structuralRow r.entry "missing fixture file: \{e}"
    Ok _ => match checkRunBuild entrypath binPath
      Err e => structuralRow r.entry e
      Ok a => match codeFailures r.codes entrypath
        Err e => structuralRow r.entry e
        Ok codeNotes =>
          let checkV = verdictOf a.checkCode
          let runV = verdictOf a.runCode
          -- A `build` ACCEPT must have produced a binary: exit 0 alone
          -- would let a `NoValue` row pin ACCEPT over a build that
          -- emitted nothing.
          let buildV =
            if a.buildCode == 0 && fileExists binPath then
              "ACCEPT"
            else
              "REJECT"
          let grade = gradeValue r.mode r.pinned a
          Graded {
            entry = r.entry,
            checkV = checkV,
            runV = runV,
            buildV = buildV,
            grade = grade,
            ok =
              checkV == r.expCheck
                && runV == r.expRun
                && buildV == r.expBuild
                && gradePasses grade
                && codeNotes == [],
            note = match codeNotes
              [] => ""
              _ => " codes=\{unwords codeNotes}",
          }

rowFailure : Graded -> Option String
rowFailure g =
  if g.ok then
    None
  else
    Some
      "  \{padRight 60 ' ' g.entry} check=\{padRight 7 ' ' g.checkV} run=\{padRight 7 ' ' g.runV} build=\{padRight 7 ' ' g.buildV} value=\{g.grade}\{g.note}"

-- The sweep grades exactly the rows `table` holds, so a graded count that
-- disagrees with `tableRows` means the sweep itself stopped visiting rows —
-- a different failure from a row that missed its pin, and one a per-row
-- failure list cannot report.
summarize : Int -> List String -> Expectation
summarize graded failures =
  if graded /= tableRows then
    fail
      "swept \{intToString graded} rows, want exactly \{intToString tableRows}"
  else
    summarizeSweep graded failures

sweep : <Exec "_", FileRead "_", IO> Expectation
sweep = sweepUnits table gradeOne rowFailure summarize

-- A row may name a secondary entry point inside a fixture directory
-- (`<dir>/control.mdk`), which grades that directory a second way; only
-- `<dir>/main.mdk` names the unit itself.
unitOf : String -> String
unitOf e = match stripSuffix "/main.mdk" e
  Some d => d
  None => e

rowEntry : Row -> String
rowEntry r = r.entry

roster : List String
roster = map unitOf (map rowEntry table)

{- | Every top-level unit of the corpus: a fixture is either one `.mdk` file
   or a whole directory. `fixtureDirs` carries the anti-vacuity floor, so a
   corpus that reads cleanly but holds nothing is an `Err` rather than a
   green run over zero units. -}
corpusUnits : <FileRead "_", IO> Result String (List String)
corpusUnits = match fixtureDirs corpus
  Err e => Err e
  Ok dirs =>
    map
      (names => sort (map basename dirs ++ filter (endsWith ".mdk") names))
      (listDir corpus)

test "the table still holds every row it is supposed to" =
  match expectUnitCount tableRows table
    Err e => fail "the verdict table \{e}"
    Ok _ => pass

test "every fixture in the corpus is wired into the table" = match corpusUnits
  Err e => fail "\{corpus}: \{e}"
  Ok units => match unrosteredUnits (n => Some n) roster units
    [] => pass
    orphans =>
      fail
        "present in \{corpus} but not wired into this gate's table: \{debug orphans}"

{- | The `s6-d1-test-body-num-default` row above type-checks that fixture's
   test bodies but never executes them. Native execution is what observes
   the literal representation each defaulted body settles on and the
   generalized local's `Float` use, so the fixture's own `cases.mdk` is run
   as well. -}
test "the closed test-body defaulting fixture's own cases run natively" =
  let cases = "\{corpus}/s6-d1-test-body-num-default/cases.mdk"
  match testAssertionCount cases ["--native"]
    Err e => fail e
    Ok n =>
      if n == 3 then
        pass
      else
        fail "\{cases}: expected 3 passing assertions, got \{intToString n}"

test "check, run and build agree with every row's verdict, value and codes" =
  sweep
